{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Kz7psXTvi9TxaewRZj44S4","type":"tool_result","content":"bot-commits.yml\nci.yml\nrelease.yml\nversion.yml\n.github/workflows/release.yml\ndocs:\nTODO.md\nlayout.md\nproposal.md\nrunbooks\nsite\n\ndocs/runbooks:\nonboard-repo.md\npages-setup.md\nrelease.md\n57c3147 ci: release workflow for GoReleaser and the Homebrew cask, inert until enabled (#177) (#362)\n5288d20 Worker test step runs make lint test (#364)\n9ecfde8 security-review aid: verified tool pins (gosec v2.30.0, gitleaks v8.30.1, semgrep 1.185.0) (#378)\nea5414a feat: Merger cover: a merge with whitespace-only or non-mechanic… (#381)\n29444b4 feat: ard init and setup, part B: ard-init.lock and the upgrade…… (#380)\n1:# ard: Automated Repository Development\n5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n166:## Open issues\n180:## Scanners\n244:**The lts scanner** (`scanners/lts`, a `sh` script plus two `jq` programs and `yq` for Helm, no build step) flags components at or near end of life using the endoflife.date v1 API (`/api/v1/products/<slug>`, one request per referenced product). A component is a product plus a release cycle. Its fingerprint is `eol:<product>:<cycle>` (for example `eol:nodejs:18`), one finding lists every referencing file, and `group` is the product. The human-owned `products.json` maps Docker image names and a few npm packages to endoflife.date slugs (the Alpine image maps to `alpine-linux`); names not in it are skipped with a stderr line. Cycles match generically: a codename's first word (`bookworm`, `jammy`), else the longest dotted prefix of the version. It reads the `toolchain` line (else the `go` directive) of `go.mod`; `engines.node` and table npm dependencies of `package.json` (lowest lower bound of the range); Dockerfile `FROM` lines (`Dockerfile`, `Dockerfile.*`, `*.Dockerfile`, `Containerfile`; global `ARG` substitution, registry and digest stripped); and `repository`/`tag` or `image: name:tag` in the `values.yaml` beside each `Chart.yaml`. Severity is `high` at or past end of life, `medium` within 90 days, `low` within 180 days, otherwise no finding. A component referenced only by floors (a `go` directive, `engines.node`, an npm range) is capped at `medium`. Titles carry the date (`Go 1.21: end of life 2024-08-13`), never \"approaching\" or \"past\", because rerate changes only severity. The scan fails closed with nothing on stdout, because an absent finding closes its issue: any API failure (including a 404 for a mapped product, which means `products.json` is stale), a non-JSON response, and a `package.json` or `values.yaml` that cannot be parsed. A missing tool exits 2 (`yq` only when a `Chart.yaml` exists). Paths with a `.git`, `vendor`, `node_modules` or `testdata` segment are skipped. Manifest text reaches the issue body only as cleaned text in code spans (control characters and backticks removed, 200 characters, at most 50 references), and repo text never reaches a URL.\n254:## Triage loop\n295:## Executors and the Reaper\n320:**Agent CLIs plug in through adapters.** Each adapter turns (prompt file, context paths, budget) into a command line and parses the exit code and, where the CLI reports them, turns and cost. For Claude Code that is `claude -p --output-format=stream-json --verbose --restricted --safe-mode --strict-mcp-config --disable-slash-commands --permission-mode=dontAsk --settings='{\"includeGitInstructions\":false,\"disableAllHooks\":true}' --tools=… --allowed-tools=… --disallowed-tools=… --model <model> --max-budget-usd <max_budget_usd> --append-system-prompt-file <role prompt> -- <message>` (`--verbose` is required by `stream-json` in print mode). Every stage (scanner, triage, worker, review) gets the same tool profile: `--tools` and `--allowed-tools` are `Read,Glob,Grep,Edit,Write,Bash`, and `--disallowed-tools` is `Bash(git commit *),Bash(git push *),Bash(git remote *),Bash(gh *)` (decided, #79). ard does not pass `--bare`: it limits the CLI to Bash, Read and Edit, and `--tools` cannot widen it (#105). The planted-config isolation `--bare` gave comes instead from `--restricted` (only managed settings and `--settings` load), `--safe-mode` (no CLAUDE.md or AGENTS.md, skills, agents, hooks, MCP servers or auto memory), `--strict-mcp-config`, `--disable-slash-commands`, and `--settings` that turns off hooks and the git status snapshot (commit subjects are third-party text). `CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_BACKGROUND_TASKS=1` is set as a fixed env var (the executor allows it) so Bash cannot detach commands. The integration tests pin each of these against a worktree with planted config and must be re-run on every `TestedVersion` bump. The tool list is not a security boundary, and the deny list is advisory, because shell indirection gets around it. The real boundaries are the missing GitHub token (Docker; see the host-mode note above, #84), the committer, which commits only `.ard/<issue#>/` from a triage or review job (other edits are discarded with the worktree), and the diff guard on worker changes. An explicit tool list from the caller (`Spec.Tools`) replaces the allow list and keeps the deny list. The user message holds only fixed text and the paths of the untrusted input files. The installed CLI accepts an undocumented `--max-turns`; ard does not rely on it, so a per-job dollar cap and the executor's hard timeout bound each run. Agents run with an empty per-job CLI config dir and HOME and receive exactly one model credential, passed by name by the executor and never visible to the adapter: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) or a subscription OAuth token (`CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, from `claude setup-token`); that rests on the environment, not on a CLI flag. `ard serve` chooses the credential from its own environment: `ARD_AGENT_AUTH` is `auto` (the default; the token if set, else the key), `oauth` or `apikey`, and an explicit mode never falls back to the other variable. Only one is ever passed, because the CLI prefers the API key and would silently bill it (pinned by an integration test). A missing credential does not stop startup, because deterministic scanners need none: `ard serve` logs one INFO line, and a job that needs one fails with a clear error. The executor creates the per-job config dir as `<job home>/.claude` (0700) in `prepare` (`Handle.View.ConfigDir`; under Docker it is a bind mount at `/ard-config`), refuses to start a job whose config dir is not that empty directory, and removes it with the job home; the Reaper's `KeepWorktree` retention keeps it too, and it holds no credential, since the credential travels only in the environment. `CLAUDE_CODE_SUBPROCESS_ENV_SCRUB=1`, which would keep the token out of Bash children, is not set (decided, #125). Residual risk: an API key (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`) is inherited by Bash children; on the tested release the CLI does not pass `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN` to them; and either credential can still be read from the `claude` process's own environment (`/proc/$PPID/environ` on Linux, `ps eww` on macOS). The container is the boundary: it holds no GitHub token, and the credential is scoped to the model. The scrub is off because on 2.1.328 it requires bubblewrap (and socat) plus unprivileged user namespaces; Docker blocks those under ard's flags (`--cap-drop ALL`, `no-new-privileges`, default seccomp), so every Bash call would fail closed and without bwrap the CLI refuses to start. On macOS host mode it worked and matched the `dontAsk` outcomes. It can be revisited for host mode or a loosened container. What agent Bash can read differs by mode: under Docker, only the job's own credential; in host mode, far more, because any same-uid process's environment is readable (`ps eww <pid>` on macOS, `/proc/<pid>/environ` on Linux), including `ard serve`'s own, which holds both credentials. The scrub would not close that; it is part of the host-mode risk of #84. `kimi` and `dsh-tui` both run headless with `-p`; neither has a turn cap and `kimi` reports no cost, so turns and cost are optional in the adapter result. `config.yaml` names the default CLI and any per-role override, so a role can move between CLIs without touching its prompt. The Claude adapter also reads a usage limit and token usage from the stream: a limit comes from a `rate_limit_event` with status `rejected` (its `resetsAt` is the reset time), from the CLI's own error text (`You've hit your session, weekly, Opus or Sonnet limit`) and from `rate_limit` or `billing_error` errors, and it records `usage` and `modelUsage` token counts (by input, output, cache write and cache read; per model when the result has `modelUsage`, which is preferred for accounting). That text is matched only on paths the CLI itself writes (an assistant message carrying an `error`, or an errored result with HTTP 429 or `api_error`), never in agent output, because an agent can be made to print the phrase. Only the job's own agent step and its fix steps are read for a limit: a worker's test step runs agent-written code, so its output is never classified (a printed limit line there cannot block the account). With an API key a 429 stays a transient `rate_limit` retry; only subscription (OAuth) runs have usage limits. The adapter also reads `rate_limit_info.unifiedWindows` from every `rate_limit_event` into `Result.Windows` (one reading per window type, the last winning, also when the run ends with no result). The controller records the readings for OAuth steps only, from the job's own agent and fix steps and never from a test step, including killed steps with no result; it logs them as `limits.reading`. A recording error is logged as `limits.observe_failed` and never fails the job. Nothing is gated on the readings yet (#63). The committed fixtures are scrubbed real captures where a human has made them (`testdata/CAPTURE.md`) and clearly named `synthetic_*` files elsewhere.\n330:1. **Who acts.** The Reaper kills only a job that a goroutine in this process still drives (its pool slot is held and was not adopted at startup), and only for a deadline or a quiet heartbeat; it records the reason first, and the owner's close-out reads it. A worker keeps its job after the agent exits (tests, fix rounds), so \"exited\" alone never means abandoned. Any job nobody drives (adopted after a restart, or no slot) is closed out by the Reaper itself, and its adopted slot is released. A job whose close-out has started is resumed, never failed.\n341:## Worker loop\n361:## Reviewer loop\n396:## Merger loop\n421:## PR watch loop\n435:## Configuration\n463:#tokens_per_day: 40M        # optional per-role daily token default (40M, 1.5M, 500k or a plain integer); a role may set its own; unset = no token cap; enforced at the claim gate with the dollar caps\n509:## Guardrails\n526:- **The tag-writing credential is separate.** Only the `tag` job of `version.yml`, in the `release` environment, holds a token that can push tags, from a release App that must differ from ard's App (the job refuses otherwise). Tags are never moved or deleted. The logic is inline bash under `.github/`, which agents cannot change, and `internal/versioncheck` tests it.\n536:## Gaps in the original sketch\n556:## Delivery\n564:- **Versioning ([#183](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/183)).** `version.yml` gives every first-parent merge to `main` an annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` and a notes-only release, oldest first. The bump comes from the merged PR's labels: `semver:major`, `semver:minor`, else patch; a direct push gets patch, and ard's own main-lane commits (committer `ARD_BOT_LOGIN`) are skipped. The seed tag is `v0.1.0`. The workflow is a dry run until the repository variable `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on`. `make build` (`git describe` limited to plain `vX.Y.Z` tags) and `go install ...@vX.Y.Z` (build info) both print the tag from `ard version`. `internal/versioncheck` tests the workflow's scripts. See `docs/runbooks/release.md`.\n565:- **Homebrew ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)).** `.github/workflows/release.yml` builds each new `v*` tag with GoReleaser (`.goreleaser.yaml`: darwin and linux, arm64 and amd64, CGO off, `-X main.version`), adds the archives and `checksums.txt` to the release `version.yml` created (`release.mode: keep-existing`) and updates the cask in `albertcbrown/homebrew-tap` with `TAP_GITHUB_TOKEN`, a token scoped to the tap and held only in the `release` environment. It stays inert until `ARD_VERSIONING` is `on` and that token exists, and on a tag push it releases only the newest tag, so a backfill publishes once. Gatekeeper: the binary is not yet signed or notarized, so the cask clears the quarantine flag after install (`hooks.post.install`), an accepted risk; notarization is a follow-up.\n566:- **Release.** An annotated tag `vX.Y.Z` (`git tag -a`) builds that commit with `VERSION=vX.Y.Z`, pushes `:vX.Y.Z` and moves `:latest` when it is the highest version. Release tags are annotated because Jenkins builds only tags at most a day old and dates a lightweight tag by its commit, so a lightweight tag on an older commit never builds. Every merge to `main` is tagged by the Versioning step above; releases do not change what is deployed, which follows `main`.\n569:## Rollout\n579:    P4 --> P5[\"Phase 5: Distribution<br/>versioned releases<br/>Homebrew cask\"]\n582:Phases 4 and 5 have no gate: Phase 4 ([#178](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/178)) writes the user documentation and publishes it as a GitHub Pages site, and Phase 5 ([#177](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/177)) publishes versioned releases and a Homebrew cask (`brew install --cask albertcbrown/tap/ard`).\n598:## Open questions\nREADME.md:1:# ard — automated repository development\nREADME.md:9:## How it works\nREADME.md:26:## Configuration by convention\nREADME.md:42:## Development\nREADME.md:54:## License\ndocs/runbooks/release.md:1:# Release runbook\ndocs/runbooks/release.md:5:## Seed tag\ndocs/runbooks/release.md:16:## Settings checklist\ndocs/runbooks/release.md:21:## Homebrew release (release.yml)\ndocs/runbooks/release.md:32:## Dry run to live\ndocs/runbooks/release.md:36:## Bumps and the module path\ndocs/runbooks/release.md:40:## Recovery","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"45379bcf-1105-43f4-9d7c-90b4cdbd049a","uuid":"6fbff751-ea7a-4daf-aa6c-75975fbeadaa","timestamp":"2026-11-25T08:50:15.621Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"bot-commits.yml\nci.yml\nrelease.yml\nversion.yml\n.github/workflows/release.yml\ndocs:\nTODO.md\nlayout.md\nproposal.md\nrunbooks\nsite\n\ndocs/runbooks:\nonboard-repo.md\npages-setup.md\nrelease.md\n57c3147 ci: release workflow for GoReleaser and the Homebrew cask, inert until enabled (#177) (#362)\n5288d20 Worker test step runs make lint test (#364)\n9ecfde8 security-review aid: verified tool pins (gosec v2.30.0, gitleaks v8.30.1, semgrep 1.185.0) (#378)\nea5414a feat: Merger cover: a merge with whitespace-only or non-mechanic… (#381)\n29444b4 feat: ard init and setup, part B: ard-init.lock and the upgrade…… (#380)\n1:# ard: Automated Repository Development\n5:## Summary\n22:## Architecture\n44:## Stack\n48:## Label state machine\n95:## The .ard/ contract\n166:## Open issues\n180:## Scanners\n244:**The lts scanner** (`scanners/lts`, a `sh` script plus two `jq` programs and `yq` for Helm, no build step) flags components at or near end of life using the endoflife.date v1 API (`/api/v1/products/<slug>`, one request per referenced product). A component is a product plus a release cycle. Its fingerprint is `eol:<product>:<cycle>` (for example `eol:nodejs:18`), one finding lists every referencing file, and `group` is the product. The human-owned `products.json` maps Docker image names and a few npm packages to endoflife.date slugs (the Alpine image maps to `alpine-linux`); names not in it are skipped with a stderr line. Cycles match generically: a codename's first word (`bookworm`, `jammy`)