{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01SN9jVQHjZDCzdu9tUMu1bR","type":"tool_result","content":"509\t## Guardrails\n510\t\n511\tAgents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit in code and permissions, not in prompts.\n512\t\n513\t- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\n514\t- **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to another repository (transferred) reads as not found. A repository that redirects (renamed or transferred) is a hard, non-transient error naming the new name when GitHub reveals it. ard does not follow a rename; point it at the new name. A deleted issue (410 Gone) also reads as not found.\n515\t- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token. The local executor does not enforce egress (see `network` under Configuration; the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296). In Kubernetes (#54) job egress is DNS and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (the #276 posture), and `pre` holds only a read-only per-job token revoked after it ends.\n516\t- **Isolated agent pod.** The agent container holds only its model credential. `pre` and `post` run as a different UID. A ValidatingAdmissionPolicy enforces `shareProcessNamespace: false` and refuses the App Secret, hostPath, a Secret mount in the agent container and an agent `runAsUser` equal to the sidecars'.\n517\t- **Result key and listener.** `post` serves one route, guarded by a per-pod 32-byte key mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, over plain HTTP (the key grants only this job's result, and ingress is limited). Ingress to job pods is allowed only from the ard-serve pod on the post port. `ard serve` has no listener or Service for this.\n518\t- **Result untar is the boundary.** The controller treats the pulled tar as hostile: `os.Root`; no hardlinks, devices, FIFOs, `..`, absolute paths, NUL, duplicates, `.git` components or case-fold collisions; modes masked; byte, entry, size and ratio caps counted on the stream.\n519\t- **Cluster.** The jobs namespace has Pod Security `restricted`; the job SA has no roles and no token; `enableServiceLinks: false`, non-root, drop ALL, RuntimeDefault seccomp, read-only root fs, `/tmp` on an emptyDir, `restartPolicy: Never`, images pinned by digest. The controller Role is namespaced: pods create/get/list/watch/delete, pods/log get, secrets create/delete, events list, no pods/exec.\n520\t- **Residual risks (Kubernetes).** The model credential sits in a per-job Secret in etcd, so etcd encryption at rest is required. The tee'd stdout is agent-writable, so the agent can forge its usage figures, the same exposure as local Docker, accepted by the owner; the exit code still comes from the kubelet; revisit when split log streams are available. Pod egress reaches the whole internet, and a `network: false` pod still allows GitHub and model API CIDRs. Pod Security `restricted` relies on the cluster's admission.\n521\t- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake label. That is why, in label mode, the controller honours the label only if the latest `labeled` event came from an actor with triage or higher permission, or from the App; otherwise it adds `ard:needs-human`. The intake label must start with `ard:`, and a label ard itself applies (`ard:needs-human`, a stage label and so on) cannot be the intake label; `ard config check` and `ard labels sync` reject it, and `ard serve` never admits through it. In both modes the issue is re-gated if its body is edited afterwards. Note that `all` mode lets any issue author start a job, so use it only on repos where that is acceptable. **The one exception to \"the issue body is human-owned untrusted input\" is ard's cost block.** ard owns only the text between its `ard:cost` markers. The admission hash (`agentjob.BodyHash`) and the `issue.md` snapshot given to agents both use the body with that block stripped (`costblock.Strip`), so whatever the hash ignores is exactly what no agent sees: text hidden from the re-gate can never reach an agent. Strip removes only the last begin-to-end block, and only when its contents parse as exactly the table ard renders (fixed header, rows of validated cells, a Total row, at most 5,000 bytes; LF or CRLF); a fake block, markers around prose, or an edited table is not stripped, so it stays visible to agents and counts in the hash and an edit re-gates as usual. A body with no valid block hashes exactly as before, so stored admissions stay valid. ard never overwrites or drops such human text: it appends its own block after it. The block's header says so (\"do not edit: an edited block is kept as text, shown to agents and re-gates the issue\"). ard's body writes bump `updated_at`.\n522\t- **Untrusted text is passed as data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text go to the agent as files the prompt labels as untrusted input, never spliced into the instructions. `issue.md` omits ard's own cost block.\n523\t- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\n524\t- **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job as `needs-human` and is not committed (see the `.ard/` contract).\n525\t- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its own prompt. `ard init` only proposes them, in a PR a human merges.\n526\t- **The tag-writing credential is separate.** Only the `tag` job of `version.yml`, in the `release` environment, holds a token that can push tags, from a release App that must differ from ard's App (the job refuses otherwise). Tags are never moved or deleted. The logic is inline bash under `.github/`, which agents cannot change, and `internal/versioncheck` tests it.\n527\t- **Merges are gated.** The merger lands a PR only when every review passed and required checks (read from the branch rules API) are green on an up-to-date branch; protected paths wait for a write-access human approval. The App bypasses `main`'s ruleset (its direct `.ard/` commits need it), so the merger's own check that required checks are green is what gates a merge, backed by the bot-commit audit.\n528\t- **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a closing keyword directly before a reference closes an issue on GitHub, so only those are rewritten, including the HTML-entity (`&#35;12`, `&#x23;12`, `&num;12`) and backslash-escaped (`\\#12`) forms, which markdown renders as `#12`; \"fixed a typo in #12's test\" is left alone. Only ard's own `Closes #<issue>` on the worker PR closes an issue.\n529\t- **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file, line and rule, never text). Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do; a literal is held.\n530\t- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n531\t- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Tokens are capped too (`tokens_per_day`, optionally per role; unset means no token cap), so a role on a model with no dollar price is capped by tokens alone. With U the tokens counted today, C the cap and W the role's largest reported job total, a candidate is refused when `U >= C` or `C - U < min(W, C)`: a role stops when what is left could not cover its worst job. Running jobs reserve nothing, and deterministic scanners are never checked. A job must pass both the dollar and the token check. If the token state cannot be read, the gate refuses with `budget state unreadable` and logs WARN `budget.read_failed`; a block is logged once a day as `budget.blocked`. The `budget` loop also posts one comment per token cap and UTC day, marked `<!-- ard:budget cap=tokens role=<kind>/<name> day=<YYYY-MM-DD> -->`, with numbers only. Counted tokens are input + output + cache write; cache reads are excluded. A job that dies without reporting tokens is not counted: its close-out comment and cost-table row say \"tokens not reported\", and a role can overshoot its token cap by such runs. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. The `budget` loop posts one comment on the status issue per capped scope (the repo, or a role) per UTC day, and records a `budget_capped` event; that event also keeps a restart from posting the notice again (a crash between comment and event can repeat one notice). The comment holds only numbers, the day and config names, no agent text and no `@`; it says the dollar figure is an estimate on a subscription and how to raise the cap (edit `budget_usd_per_day` in `.ard/config.yaml` through a reviewed PR). Under `--dry-run` the comment goes to the ledger. `--loops budget` selects the notice loop; the gate is part of the pool and is always on.\n532\t- **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n533\t- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n534\t- **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the job's comment, PR) with a Total row, written by the `costs` loop. The digest is a `digest` loop that posts one new comment a day on the status issue, titled \"daily digest for <date> (UTC)\", covering the previous complete UTC day: open-issue counts per `ard:` label, jobs run, failures (with a per-role split) and spend. A `digest` event written after the post is the once-per-day marker, so a restart does not repost; it holds numbers, label and role names only. A reached daily cap is a `budget_capped` event. The repository may be public, so the spend it shows is public too; the table holds no agent text or host paths. Scanner failure streaks (#221, the early slice of the digest, #58): each scanner posts one comment on the status issue when a failure streak starts and one when it recovers. A streak starts at the first run that is a guard trip or guard error (reason: the guard rule name or `scan-error`), any other `scan_failed` run (a `timeout` counts as `scan_failed`) or a skip at `max_open`, and ends at the next `ok` run. The posted class is kept in the scanner's `meta` table (key `failure_streak`), so a restart does not repost, and it is written only after the post succeeds. Runs dropped at the global cap and `error` runs neither start nor end a streak. A comment carries the scanner name and the reason class only, never finding text, titles, fingerprints or log lines. The post goes through the effect layer, so a dry-run logs it (`scanner.streak_notice`) and stores no streak. While the status issue number is unknown nothing is posted and the streak is not recorded, so it posts once the issue exists.\n535\t\n536\t## Gaps in the original sketch\n537\t\n538\tThirteen points in the first sketch would break or loop in practice; each has a fix above."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"45379bcf-1105-43f4-9d7c-90b4cdbd049a","uuid":"57ef29f2-27c4-4f86-965a-615ea454e7ca","timestamp":"2026-11-25T08:50:18.371Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/work/docs/proposal.md","content":"## Guardrails\n\nAgents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit in code and permissions, not in prompts.\n\n- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\n- **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to another repository (transferred) reads as not found. A repository that redirects (renamed or transferred) is a hard, non-transient error naming the new name when GitHub reveals it. ard does not follow a rename; point it at the new name. A deleted issue (410 Gone) also reads as not found.\n- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token. The local executor does not enforce egress (see `network` under Configuration; the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296). In Kubernetes (#54) job egress is DNS and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (the #276 posture), and `pre` holds only a read-only per-job token revoked after it ends.\n- **Isolated agent pod.** The agent container holds only its model credential. `pre` and `post` run as a different UID. A ValidatingAdmissionPolicy enforces `shareProcessNamespace: false` and refuses the App Secret, hostPath, a Secret mount in the agent container and an agent `runAsUser` equal to the sidecars'.\n- **Result key and listener.** `post` serves one route, guarded by a per-pod 32-byte key mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, over plain HTTP (the key grants only this job's result, and ingress is limited). Ingress to job pods is allowed only from the ard-serve pod on the post port. `ard serve` has no listener or Service for this.\n- **Result untar is the boundary.** The controller treats the pulled tar as hostile: `os.Root`; no hardlinks, devices, FIFOs, `..`, absolute paths, NUL, duplicates, `.git` components or case-fold collisions; modes masked; byte, entry, size and ratio caps counted on the stream.\n- **Cluster.** The jobs namespace has Pod Security `restricted`; the job SA has no roles and no token; `enableServiceLinks: false`, non-root, drop ALL, RuntimeDefault seccomp, read-only root fs, `/tmp` on an emptyDir, `restartPolicy: Never`, images pinned by digest. The controller Role is namespaced: pods create/get/list/watch/delete, pods/log get, secrets create/delete, events list, no pods/exec.\n- **Residual risks (Kubernetes).** The model credential sits in a per-job Secret in etcd, so etcd encryption at rest is required. The tee'd stdout is agent-writable, so the agent can forge its usage figures, the same exposure as local Docker, accepted by the owner; the exit code still comes from the kubelet; revisit when split log streams are available. Pod egress reaches the whole internet, and a `network: false` pod still allows GitHub and model API CIDRs. Pod Security `restricted` relies on the cluster's admission.\n- **Intake is configurable (decided, #2).** `intake` is either `all` (every human-filed issue enters, no label) or `label` (only issues with the configured label). Issue templates and forms apply their labels on the author's behalf, so an outsider can file an issue that already carries the intake label. That is why, in label mode, the controller honours the label only if the latest `labeled` event came from an actor with triage or higher permission, or from the App; otherwise it adds `ard:needs-human`. The intake label must start with `ard:`, and a label ard itself applies (`ard:needs-human`, a stage label and so on) cannot be the intake label; `ard config check` and `ard labels sync` reject it, and `ard serve` never admits through it. In both modes the issue is re-gated if its body is edited afterwards. Note that `all` mode lets any issue author start a job, so use it only on repos where that is acceptable. **The one exception to \"the issue body is human-owned untrusted input\" is ard's cost block.** ard owns only the text between its `ard:cost` markers. The admission hash (`agentjob.BodyHash`) and the `issue.md` snapshot given to agents both use the body with that block stripped (`costblock.Strip`), so whatever the hash ignores is exactly what no agent sees: text hidden from the re-gate can never reach an agent. Strip removes only the last begin-to-end block, and only when its contents parse as exactly the table ard renders (fixed header, rows of validated cells, a Total row, at most 5,000 bytes; LF or CRLF); a fake block, markers around prose, or an edited table is not stripped, so it stays visible to agents and counts in the hash and an edit re-gates as usual. A body with no valid block hashes exactly as before, so stored admissions stay valid. ard never overwrites or drops such human text: it appends its own block after it. The block's header says so (\"do not edit: an edited block is kept as text, shown to agents and re-gates the issue\"). ard's body writes bump `updated_at`.\n- **Untrusted text is passed as data.** Issue bodies, comments and PR text go to the agent as files the prompt labels as untrusted input, never spliced into the instructions. `issue.md` omits ard's own cost block.\n- **Planted repo config does not steer agents.** A branch can carry CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md, `.claude/` settings, hooks, skills, agents or a `.mcp.json`. The Claude adapter's flags stop all of them loading (see Executors). Residual risk: auth isolation comes from the empty per-job config dir and HOME, not a flag (the CLI reads no Keychain entry but the one keyed to the per-job dir); a host-mode job on macOS still runs as the operator's uid, so agent Bash could call `security` against the operator's login Keychain, including Claude's own entry, which is within the host-mode risk of #84 (a Dockerfile role or a dedicated macOS user for `ard serve` closes it); the subscription token is a one-year, model-only credential, rotated by running `claude setup-token` again and restarting `ard serve`; `--max-budget-usd` and `total_cost_usd` are estimates on a subscription; part of the block (AGENTS.md, and CLAUDE.md under `--restricted`) is observed behaviour that holds only on the tested CLI version and is re-checked by the integration tests; built-in plugins and the host's managed settings still apply; system reminders and fixed CLI prompt text are back; isolation of settings `env` is not pinned by a test, since no Bash call is scripted; Bash can still read anything the process can (host mode, #84); and an agent can rewrite CLAUDE.md or `.claude/` in its worktree, which later runs ignore but a worker PR could carry into the target repo (the `ard init` protected-path defaults cover `CLAUDE.md`, `AGENTS.md`, `.claude/` and `.mcp.json`, so such a PR needs a human).\n- **Agents write only their own files.** Close-out lets a job change under `.ard/<issue#>/` only its own role file and what its role owns (a `plan.md` section, `design/`); anything else fails the job as `needs-human` and is not committed (see the `.ard/` contract).\n- **Role files are human-owned.** The diff guard and the committer's code lane reject any worker change under `.ard/` or `.github/` (so `.ard/config.yaml`, `.ard/PRODUCT.md`, `.ard/VISION.md`, `.ard/TECH_STACK.md` and every role directory), and the merger treats those paths as protected through `merge.protected_paths` (the `ard init` defaults), so no agent can rewrite its own prompt. `ard init` only proposes them, in a PR a human merges.\n- **The tag-writing credential is separate.** Only the `tag` job of `version.yml`, in the `release` environment, holds a token that can push tags, from a release App that must differ from ard's App (the job refuses otherwise). Tags are never moved or deleted. The logic is inline bash under `.github/`, which agents cannot change, and `internal/versioncheck` tests it.\n- **Merges are gated.** The merger lands a PR only when every review passed and required checks (read from the branch rules API) are green on an up-to-date branch; protected paths wait for a write-access human approval. The App bypasses `main`'s ruleset (its direct `.ard/` commits need it), so the merger's own check that required checks are green is what gates a merge, backed by the bot-commit audit.\n- **Agent text cannot close issues.** Agent text in a commit message, PR title or PR body has closing references neutralised (`committer.NeutraliseRefs`: \"Fixes #12\" becomes \"Fixes issue 12\"). Only a closing keyword directly before a reference closes an issue on GitHub, so only those are rewritten, including the HTML-entity (`&#35;12`, `&#x23;12`, `&num;12`) and backslash-escaped (`\\#12`) forms, which markdown renders as `#12`; \"fixed a typo in #12's test\" is left alone. Only ard's own `Closes #<issue>` on the worker PR closes an issue.\n- **Worker diffs are scanned.** The worker's diff is checked for known credential values and for secret patterns on the lines the job added. A hit holds the change for a human (the comment names file, line and rule, never text). Test fixtures that look like credentials must be built at run time, as `internal/redact`'s tests do; a literal is held.\n- **Committed output is redacted.** Close-out strips known secret values, and replaces the controller host's paths (see \"All agent output is committed too\") with placeholders, in each log and every `.ard/<issue#>/` file it commits, and runs a secret scan before anything is committed or posted. A hit on a log keeps that log host-local and adds `ard:needs-human`; a hit on a role file or `plan.md` commits only the clean log and adds `ard:needs-human`. Comments never quote text the scan flagged (and the same scan runs on worker code before it is pushed, see above, and the worker's PR body and code commit message get the same secret and host-path replacement; the config-load error posted to the status issue gets the host-path replacement too), never forge an `ard:` marker and never `@`-mention anyone. The scan fails closed: if it cannot run, nothing is committed. An optional second pass runs a pinned gitleaks binary (`redact.gitleaks`, see \"All agent output is committed too\"); it is off by default, fails closed, and a hit is handled like an internal hit. Once a log's redacted copy is committed, close-out deletes the raw host log; if redaction failed or the scan flagged it, the raw log is kept (and the keep logged) for a human to inspect.\n- **Budgets.** Every job has a dollar cap (`max_budget_usd`) and a hard timeout; every role and the whole repo have a daily dollar cap. The caps are enforced at the pool's claim gate (`internal/budget`): a candidate is refused when `spent >= cap`, where spent is the cost recorded in `step_usage` since 00:00 UTC today. The caps reset at 00:00 UTC and survive a restart, because spend is read from the controller database. A role's own `budget_usd_per_day` caps that role (by stage and name) alone; a role without one is limited only by the repo cap. Steps that reported no cost count as $0, and the refusal reason shows how many there were. There is no exemption for reviewers, the caps apply to OAuth credentials too, and deterministic scanners (no model, no cost) are exempt. Tokens are capped too (`tokens_per_day`, optionally per role; unset means no token cap), so a role on a model with no dollar price is capped by tokens alone. With U the tokens counted today, C the cap and W the role's largest reported job total, a candidate is refused when `U >= C` or `C - U < min(W, C)`: a role stops when what is left could not cover its worst job. Running jobs reserve nothing, and deterministic scanners are never checked. A job must pass both the dollar and the token check. If the token state cannot be read, the gate refuses with `budget state unreadable` and logs WARN `budget.read_failed`; a block is logged once a day as `budget.blocked`. The `budget` loop also posts one comment per token cap and UTC day, marked `<!-- ard:budget cap=tokens role=<kind>/<name> day=<YYYY-MM-DD> -->`, with numbers only. Counted tokens are input + output + cache write; cache reads are excluded. A job that dies without reporting tokens is not counted: its close-out comment and cost-table row say \"tokens not reported\", and a role can overshoot its token cap by such runs. Running jobs are never killed for a cap. If today's spend cannot be read, the gate refuses (fails closed). Each cap is logged once a day as `budget.capped`. The `budget` loop posts one comment on the status issue per capped scope (the repo, or a role) per UTC day, and records a `budget_capped` event; that event also keeps a restart from posting the notice again (a crash between comment and event can repeat one notice). The comment holds only numbers, the day and config names, no agent text and no `@`; it says the dollar figure is an estimate on a subscription and how to raise the cap (edit `budget_usd_per_day` in `.ard/config.yaml` through a reviewed PR). Under `--dry-run` the comment goes to the ledger. `--loops budget` selects the notice loop; the gate is part of the pool and is always on.\n- **Concurrency.** `max_pods` (default 3) caps every job together, and at most one triage job runs per issue at a time, because triage roles share `plan.md`; no triage job is claimed for an issue while a worker job runs on it.\n- **Kill switch.** Four sources stop new claims, scanner runs (filing and autoclose), stale closes and merges: a local pause file `~/.ard/pause/<owner>/<repo>` outside the repository (the path is lowercased; `ard serve` creates the directory, so `touch` pauses and `rm` resumes), `paused: true` in the `config.yaml` in the host's working tree, `ard:pause` on the pinned status issue, and `paused: true` in the config on `origin/main`. They are checked cheapest first, before every claiming tick. The delay is at most one loop interval plus 10% jitter; a tick already running may finish its claims. `SIGUSR1` (Unix only) makes every loop run one tick now, logged as `controller.wake`; the tick still passes the kill switch and the job pool, and wakes that arrive while a tick runs coalesce into one extra tick. The gate answers one controller-wide question and has no per-kind checks, so every loop that writes (scanner filing and autoclose, stale closes, merges, claims), except the Reaper, the cost-table writer and in-flight close-outs, must report `Claims() == true` and is skipped while paused. Running jobs, in-flight close-outs, the Reaper and the `costs` loop continue: the table is bookkeeping about work already done, not new work. The check fails closed: an unreadable status issue, a failed fetch of `origin/main`, or a config that fails to load counts as paused. The pause file pauses when anything exists at the path (a file, a directory or a symlink; content is ignored), and a path that cannot be examined, for example because `pause/<owner>` is a regular file, also counts as paused. The local working-tree `config.yaml` is read only for its `paused` key; a missing, unreadable or invalid file does not pause by itself, but a readable one that says `paused: true` does. The pause file and the local `paused` are the kill switches that nothing pushed to GitHub can lift. Config and role directories otherwise come from `origin/main` at the same SHA the tick's jobs use (see Configuration). `ard serve` remembers the status issue number in `_controller.db` (`meta` key `status_issue`). At startup it reads that issue directly and adopts it if it is still open, titled `ard status`, carries the `<!-- ard:status -->` marker and has the App as author; otherwise it searches by those same tests, and creates and pins the issue if none matches. Search is eventually consistent, so the remembered number is what stops a quick restart from creating a second issue; if two still match, the lowest number wins. A cached issue that cannot be read for a reason other than not-found falls back to search rather than aborting startup. A dry-run never stores its synthetic (negative) number; a real number found by search is stored only in the separate dry-run database. A second `ard serve` on the same repo exits with an error: the leader lock is an `flock` on `~/.ard/locks/<owner>/<repo>.lock` (local filesystem only), shared by live and dry-run.\n- **Observability.** Every claim, close-out and failure is an event in local state. A daily digest on the pinned status issue shows queue depth by label, jobs run, failures and spend. Each issue's body also carries a per-job cost table (agent, tokens by type, cost, model, outcome and a link to the job's comment, PR) with a Total row, written by the `costs` loop. The digest is a `digest` loop that posts one new comment a day on the status issue, titled \"daily digest for <date> (UTC)\", covering the previous complete UTC day: open-issue counts per `ard:` label, jobs run, failures (with a per-role split) and spend. A `digest` event written after the post is the once-per-day marker, so a restart does not repost; it holds numbers, label and role names only. A reached daily cap is a `budget_capped` event. The repository may be public, so the spend it shows is public too; the table holds no agent text or host paths. Scanner failure streaks (#221, the early slice of the digest, #58): each scanner posts one comment on the status issue when a failure streak starts and one when it recovers. A streak starts at the first run that is a guard trip or guard error (reason: the guard rule name or `scan-error`), any other `scan_failed` run (a `timeout` counts as `scan_failed`) or a skip at `max_open`, and ends at the next `ok` run. The posted class is kept in the scanner's `meta` table (key `failure_streak`), so a restart does not repost, and it is written only after the post succeeds. Runs dropped at the global cap and `error` runs neither start nor end a streak. A comment carries the scanner name and the reason class only, never finding text, titles, fingerprints or log lines. The post goes through the effect layer, so a dry-run logs it (`scanner.streak_notice`) and stores no streak. While the status issue number is unknown nothing is posted and the streak is not recorded, so it posts once the issue exists.\n\n## Gaps in the original sketch\n\nThirteen points in the first sketch would break or loop in practice; each has a fix above.","numLines":30,"startLine":509,"totalLines":607}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01SN9jVQHjZDCzdu9tUMu1bR","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
